      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\n\nFixes a test in `tests/distributed/test_pynccl.py` which would switch to AITER implementation but not properly capture the graph as a result. Disables custom all_reduce functions.\n\n## Claims\n\n\nFixes a failing test case when AITER is on.\n\n\n## Validation\n\n\n```\nVLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=1 python3 -m pytest -v -s \\\n  tests/distributed/test_pynccl.py::test_pynccl_multiple_allreduce_with_vllm\n```\n## Details\nAdd `set_custom_all_reduce(False)` to `multiple_allreduce_with_vllm_worker_fn`. \n\nReason is that this file was intended to test pynccl not custom all_reduce functions. This is evident in the size of the tensor which is fp32 and size 16*1024*1024 elements (64MB). vLLM custom all_reduce does not handle sizes this large. So it intentionally used pynccl. But AITER supports sizes up to 64MB by default.\n\nA separate test will be implemented in a future PR that can test AITER's behavior in this fashion.\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
